WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.241 --> 00:00:02.348
- When you find something you're good at,

00:00:02.348 --> 00:00:03.286
go for it.

00:00:03.286 --> 00:00:04.757
It's not about me being wonderful.

00:00:04.757 --> 00:00:06.315
- No, no, no, I have
that title, thank you.

00:00:06.315 --> 00:00:08.300
- It's about making a
business decision, right?

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:10.103
- Hi, I'm Mark Cuban,
I'm a bully, look at me.

00:00:10.103 --> 00:00:11.630
- What's that about, Mark?

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:14.701
- [Announcer] Team Cuban
going for it, Bjergsn, oh!

00:00:14.701 --> 00:00:16.164
The three man pulling!

00:00:16.164 --> 00:00:18.620
- Hello Believe Nation,
I'm Evan Carmichael.

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:20.188
My one word is believe,

00:00:20.188 --> 00:00:21.831
and I believe that entrepreneurs

00:00:21.831 --> 00:00:25.084
are going to solve all of the
major problems of the world.

00:00:25.084 --> 00:00:26.418
So to help you on your journey,

00:00:26.418 --> 00:00:27.914
today we're going to
learn from billionaire

00:00:27.914 --> 00:00:29.987
Shark Tank investor, Mark Cuban,

00:00:29.987 --> 00:00:33.733
and my take on his top 10
rules for success, volume two.

00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:35.250
And as always guys, as you're watching,

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:37.315
if you hear something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:37.315 --> 00:00:39.224
please leave it down
in the comments below.

00:00:39.224 --> 00:00:41.652
Put quotes around it so
other people can be inspired

00:00:41.652 --> 00:00:44.138
and when you write it
down it's much more likely

00:00:44.138 --> 00:00:46.940
to stick for yourself as well.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:47.773
Enjoy.

00:00:47.773 --> 00:00:50.684
(paper plane swooshes)

00:00:50.684 --> 00:00:54.434
(inspirational upbeat music)

00:01:00.743 --> 00:01:02.514
- Sometimes, being
young and trying things,

00:01:02.514 --> 00:01:04.284
you're so naive and you
don't know any better,

00:01:04.284 --> 00:01:05.717
all you do is learn.

00:01:05.717 --> 00:01:07.620
And, if you fail it doesn't matter.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:11.454
And so, whether I was
nine, 10, 12, 16, 21,

00:01:11.454 --> 00:01:13.791
the failures were irrelevant.

00:01:13.791 --> 00:01:18.274
And whether you're nine,
12, 16, 21, 22, 24,

00:01:18.274 --> 00:01:20.415
I'm sleeping on the couch.

00:01:20.415 --> 00:01:23.115
I have a car with a
hole in the floorboard.

00:01:23.115 --> 00:01:26.208
I'm living like a bum and like a student.

00:01:26.208 --> 00:01:27.564
And so what did I have to lose?

00:01:27.564 --> 00:01:30.711
And so, I think that influenced
me as much as anything.

00:01:30.711 --> 00:01:32.770
So, my dad was like,

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:35.121
"Go for it. You know? Why not?

00:01:35.121 --> 00:01:36.485
"What have you got to lose?

00:01:36.485 --> 00:01:38.549
"You have to sleep on a different couch?"

00:01:38.549 --> 00:01:41.027
So I think that was motivation.

00:01:41.027 --> 00:01:41.989
The type of person you are

00:01:41.989 --> 00:01:44.391
kind of points to the
things you're good at.

00:01:44.391 --> 00:01:45.857
Are you 12? You're not going to know.

00:01:45.857 --> 00:01:47.666
- Are you 16? You're not going to know.

00:01:47.666 --> 00:01:51.074
If you're 18, maybe if you
have a special talent, right?

00:01:51.074 --> 00:01:51.907
- Yeah.

00:01:51.907 --> 00:01:53.582
- You can throw a baseball
a hundred miles an hour.

00:01:53.582 --> 00:01:55.878
You can sing and your voice is incredible.

00:01:55.878 --> 00:01:58.058
You're a concert pianist.

00:01:58.058 --> 00:01:59.573
You're a chess Grandmaster.

00:01:59.573 --> 00:02:01.404
If you have a specific skill set or talent

00:02:01.404 --> 00:02:03.493
that just immediately
propels you to the top,

00:02:03.493 --> 00:02:04.675
yeah you want to follow that.

00:02:04.675 --> 00:02:05.508
- Yeah.

00:02:05.508 --> 00:02:07.042
- But I remember having
a list, I still have it.

00:02:07.042 --> 00:02:09.140
When I graduated from college,

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:10.333
I was getting ready to graduate,

00:02:10.333 --> 00:02:11.902
and all of the different industries

00:02:11.902 --> 00:02:14.148
that I thought I might want to partake in.

00:02:14.148 --> 00:02:15.717
When you find something you're good at.

00:02:15.717 --> 00:02:16.550
- Yeah.

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:17.448
- Go for it.

00:02:17.448 --> 00:02:20.379
I was never really into
technology when I was a kid.

00:02:20.379 --> 00:02:22.950
But then as I got a job that used it,

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:24.195
I found out I was good at it.

00:02:24.195 --> 00:02:26.213
And once I found out I was good at it,

00:02:26.213 --> 00:02:27.995
then it became a lot more fun.

00:02:27.995 --> 00:02:28.899
You know, when you're good at something

00:02:28.899 --> 00:02:30.587
it's almost always fun, right?
- Yeah, yeah.

00:02:30.587 --> 00:02:31.718
- And once it was fun,

00:02:31.718 --> 00:02:33.849
and I combined that
with my business skills,

00:02:33.849 --> 00:02:35.007
then I saw, okay,

00:02:35.007 --> 00:02:36.625
I know how to make money at this stuff.

00:02:36.625 --> 00:02:37.755
Cause I know how to sell.

00:02:37.755 --> 00:02:38.865
Now I know tech.

00:02:38.865 --> 00:02:40.546
That gives me something I love to do.

00:02:40.546 --> 00:02:42.219
And so, what I tell people,

00:02:42.219 --> 00:02:45.310
the key is find something you
love to do and be great at it.

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:48.585
Because once you do that,
it's all gravy from there.

00:02:48.585 --> 00:02:51.075
What if I offered you 1.25 million dollars

00:02:51.075 --> 00:02:52.214
to sell the whole company.

00:02:52.214 --> 00:02:54.846
- Mark, you're saying,
"Screw the other Sharks."

00:02:54.846 --> 00:02:55.900
- Yup.

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:57.365
I like what you guys are doing.

00:02:57.365 --> 00:02:59.441
I want you to come back
to me with a percentage.

00:02:59.441 --> 00:03:01.076
I don't want you to
even talk to these guys.

00:03:01.076 --> 00:03:04.187
- You are asking him to take
that option with you right now

00:03:04.187 --> 00:03:05.495
and ignore us?
- Yup.

00:03:05.495 --> 00:03:06.620
- You're not a very nice guy.

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:07.890
- It's not about me being wonderful.

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:09.615
- No, no, I have that title, thank you.

00:03:09.615 --> 00:03:11.467
- It's about making a
business decision, right?

00:03:11.467 --> 00:03:12.300
- Hi, I'm Mark Cuban.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:13.576
I'm a bully. Look at me.

00:03:13.576 --> 00:03:14.798
- What's that about, Mark?

00:03:14.798 --> 00:03:17.007
- Like I said, guys,
you got to pay to play.

00:03:17.007 --> 00:03:19.095
- There's tension in
this room, I can feel it.

00:03:19.095 --> 00:03:19.952
- It's offensive.

00:03:19.952 --> 00:03:21.152
- You got a shot here, right?

00:03:21.152 --> 00:03:22.384
Don't let these guys drag you down.

00:03:22.384 --> 00:03:23.810
- Are you really interested in this?

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:24.819
- I like the idea.

00:03:24.819 --> 00:03:28.306
- You really think cooking
bacon bedside, is a good idea?

00:03:28.306 --> 00:03:29.643
- I love your passion.

00:03:29.643 --> 00:03:30.794
I love your knowledge.

00:03:30.794 --> 00:03:31.627
- I'm waiting for the but.

00:03:31.627 --> 00:03:32.735
- Yeah, there's a but coming.

00:03:32.735 --> 00:03:34.243
- You've got a 24 second shot clock.

00:03:34.243 --> 00:03:35.076
$750,000.

00:03:36.137 --> 00:03:37.141
- I've never seen greed like that.

00:03:37.141 --> 00:03:38.289
That's unbelievable greed.

00:03:38.289 --> 00:03:39.702
- You broke up with your potential partner

00:03:39.702 --> 00:03:40.832
without even looking at him.

00:03:40.832 --> 00:03:41.951
- You're a savage.

00:03:41.951 --> 00:03:45.262
- I'm just a lean, mean,
shark machine, baby!

00:03:45.262 --> 00:03:47.714
If I say yes to whatever your counter is,

00:03:47.714 --> 00:03:49.963
you have to tell me that
you will say yes immediately

00:03:49.963 --> 00:03:51.013
and we have a deal.

00:03:51.013 --> 00:03:52.162
- $100,000.

00:03:52.162 --> 00:03:52.996
- Wait a minute.

00:03:52.996 --> 00:03:53.995
- For 20% equity.

00:03:53.995 --> 00:03:54.828
- Ahhh!

00:03:54.828 --> 00:03:56.292
- Done, done, and done.
- Crap.

00:03:56.292 --> 00:03:57.345
- You like that Barbara?

00:03:57.345 --> 00:03:58.546
- I don't like it one bit.

00:03:58.546 --> 00:04:00.404
(Mark laughs)
That was so damn smart.

00:04:00.404 --> 00:04:01.930
- No but that's a compliment to you.

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:04.544
- Barbara, I screwed you but
it's a compliment to you.

00:04:04.544 --> 00:04:05.860
- I feel really good about it.

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:08.734
- This isn't the rose
garden, it's the Shark Tank.

00:04:08.734 --> 00:04:09.602
And you know what,

00:04:09.602 --> 00:04:10.960
sometimes you got to take the bite

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:12.225
and sometimes you're the chum.

00:04:12.225 --> 00:04:14.238
You just have to try different things

00:04:14.238 --> 00:04:17.071
and even if 99% of them fail,

00:04:18.039 --> 00:04:20.886
you only have to be right one time.

00:04:20.886 --> 00:04:23.645
You don't have to figure
it all out in advance.

00:04:23.645 --> 00:04:24.760
You can be wrong.

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:26.724
You can pick the wrong career

00:04:26.724 --> 00:04:28.158
or you could pick the wrong job.

00:04:28.158 --> 00:04:30.240
You can pick the wrong spouse.

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:32.007
You could pick the wrong whatever,

00:04:32.007 --> 00:04:35.391
but, you get it right
one time, you're set.

00:04:35.391 --> 00:04:36.957
But if you don't try,

00:04:36.957 --> 00:04:37.946
and if you don't go out there

00:04:37.946 --> 00:04:38.978
and try all those different things,

00:04:38.978 --> 00:04:40.511
you'll never get that one time.

00:04:40.511 --> 00:04:42.656
And so, it's not a question of,

00:04:42.656 --> 00:04:44.856
okay, how much different stuff do I do?

00:04:44.856 --> 00:04:46.184
Or how many different
things should you try?

00:04:46.184 --> 00:04:47.372
Try 'em all.

00:04:47.372 --> 00:04:50.054
Try 'em all until you
find the one you like.

00:04:50.054 --> 00:04:52.298
And then it's like, it gets really easy.

00:04:52.298 --> 00:04:56.396
Cause even when (mumbles)
up, you're on a mission.

00:04:56.396 --> 00:04:57.420
You're loving what you do.

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:58.404
You know?

00:04:58.404 --> 00:04:59.920
And then it gets easy.

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:00.996
And so, that's just the approach

00:05:00.996 --> 00:05:02.159
I've always tried to instill

00:05:02.159 --> 00:05:05.410
in people who work for me and myself.

00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:07.830
I knew I was wired to be
excited about business.

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:09.880
How or why?
I don't know.

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:12.759
But there are certain
guys who have the genetics

00:05:12.759 --> 00:05:14.372
to jump out at the gym, right?

00:05:14.372 --> 00:05:15.205
- Mhmm, mhmm.

00:05:15.205 --> 00:05:16.727
- There are certain guys
that when they golf,

00:05:16.727 --> 00:05:19.717
they have the muscle
memory and the discipline.

00:05:19.717 --> 00:05:23.219
Dirk Nowitzki may not be the
most talented guy in the NBA,

00:05:23.219 --> 00:05:25.004
but his discipline and his focus

00:05:25.004 --> 00:05:27.163
to do what's necessary to be successful,

00:05:27.163 --> 00:05:28.288
he's willing to do.

00:05:28.288 --> 00:05:32.485
And combine it with being seven
feet tall and being skilled,

00:05:32.485 --> 00:05:34.394
makes him an amazing basketball player.

00:05:34.394 --> 00:05:37.417
So, it's understanding
what your skill set is,

00:05:37.417 --> 00:05:40.090
finding the right place
to use those skills,

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:41.779
and then going for it.

00:05:41.779 --> 00:05:43.751
Will that make you 250 grand?

00:05:43.751 --> 00:05:45.390
It depends if you pick the right industry.

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:47.305
But whatever industry you pick,

00:05:47.305 --> 00:05:48.702
if you out-work everybody,

00:05:48.702 --> 00:05:51.329
if you try to be a little
smarter than everybody,

00:05:51.329 --> 00:05:53.771
if you try to be a better
salesperson than everybody,

00:05:53.771 --> 00:05:56.357
if you try to be better
prepared than everybody,

00:05:56.357 --> 00:05:57.701
you've got your best chance.

00:05:57.701 --> 00:06:01.519
Because if you don't do it,
and somebody else does...

00:06:01.519 --> 00:06:02.352
You know, I have this saying,

00:06:02.352 --> 00:06:04.592
"Work like someone's trying
to take it all away from you."

00:06:04.592 --> 00:06:07.760
Work, actually work like
someone's working 24 hours

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:09.673
to take it all away from you,

00:06:09.673 --> 00:06:11.593
and that's kind of the way I look at it.

00:06:11.593 --> 00:06:14.093
I remember asking my dad to...

00:06:15.324 --> 00:06:16.653
I wanted new basketball shoes,

00:06:16.653 --> 00:06:18.311
'cause I was a basketball junky back then.

00:06:18.311 --> 00:06:19.763
He's like,
"Well, your shoes work.

00:06:19.763 --> 00:06:21.265
"If you want a new pair of tennis shoes,

00:06:21.265 --> 00:06:22.915
"you have to go out there and get a job."

00:06:22.915 --> 00:06:25.213
And I'm like,
"Dad, I'm 12 years old."

00:06:25.213 --> 00:06:27.984
And it just so happens he was
playing poker with his buddies

00:06:27.984 --> 00:06:29.845
and one of his buddies was like,

00:06:29.845 --> 00:06:31.141
"Well, I got a job for you.

00:06:31.141 --> 00:06:32.955
"I've got these garbage
bags that we distribute.

00:06:32.955 --> 00:06:34.567
"You can sell them door to door."

00:06:34.567 --> 00:06:38.148
I'm like, okay, and it was
when I was selling them

00:06:38.148 --> 00:06:40.970
and realizing that I like to sell

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:42.637
and that I could sell

00:06:42.637 --> 00:06:46.131
and that I recognized
that selling was about

00:06:46.131 --> 00:06:48.599
providing a service and
creating value for people,

00:06:48.599 --> 00:06:51.662
that I knew, literally, back then,

00:06:51.662 --> 00:06:53.761
I knew that I could always succeed.

00:06:53.761 --> 00:06:56.178
I remember, I was 16 I think,

00:06:57.293 --> 00:06:59.996
when I started a stamp company,

00:06:59.996 --> 00:07:03.214
and started going to stamp
shows and trade shows,

00:07:03.214 --> 00:07:05.653
and just working a little
bit harder than other people

00:07:05.653 --> 00:07:08.407
and trading up from one stamp to the next.

00:07:08.407 --> 00:07:10.891
I remember one time I
started with a quarter

00:07:10.891 --> 00:07:12.876
and bought a stamp and left with $50

00:07:12.876 --> 00:07:16.249
thinking, "hey if I could do
this, I could do anything."

00:07:16.249 --> 00:07:17.855
And it's not that everything worked.

00:07:17.855 --> 00:07:19.362
I failed a lot.

00:07:19.362 --> 00:07:20.998
But I never ever felt

00:07:20.998 --> 00:07:25.123
like I wouldn't be able to
work hard enough to succeed.

00:07:25.123 --> 00:07:27.156
I also always say, it's not about passion.

00:07:27.156 --> 00:07:29.623
Everybody's got passion
for something, right?

00:07:29.623 --> 00:07:32.791
Don't follow your passions,
follow your efforts.

00:07:32.791 --> 00:07:35.133
Because, people say, "I was
passionate to play baseball."

00:07:35.133 --> 00:07:36.747
"I was passionate to play basketball."

00:07:36.747 --> 00:07:38.878
Doesn't mean all of a sudden
I was going to be good enough.

00:07:38.878 --> 00:07:39.711
- Yeah.

00:07:39.711 --> 00:07:42.925
- But I found myself being
really curious about business,

00:07:42.925 --> 00:07:45.097
being really curious about technology.

00:07:45.097 --> 00:07:47.380
And that curiosity is
really what drove me.

00:07:47.380 --> 00:07:49.253
And I think, particularly
if you're young today,

00:07:49.253 --> 00:07:50.704
curiosity is great.

00:07:50.704 --> 00:07:52.881
Always learning and
trying to find new things,

00:07:52.881 --> 00:07:54.116
and being curious about new things.

00:07:54.116 --> 00:07:55.838
Cause that's what leads you to that path.

00:07:55.838 --> 00:07:58.066
Trying to figure out in advance?

00:07:58.066 --> 00:07:59.884
That's hard, cause you don't
know what you don't know.

00:07:59.884 --> 00:08:01.940
Be prepared. Understand.

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:04.070
Put yourselves in the shoes of the people

00:08:04.070 --> 00:08:06.145
or the businesses that
you're working with.

00:08:06.145 --> 00:08:08.065
The message that I just got on Cyber Dust

00:08:08.065 --> 00:08:09.386
is a perfect example.

00:08:09.386 --> 00:08:11.452
If he were to put himself in my shoes,

00:08:11.452 --> 00:08:15.284
it would be obvious that
Cyber Dust is important to me.

00:08:15.284 --> 00:08:17.657
I'm trying to push everybody
to message using it.

00:08:17.657 --> 00:08:19.524
I've volunteered, and Dave mentioned,

00:08:19.524 --> 00:08:21.526
that hey, I'm going to
be answering questions

00:08:21.526 --> 00:08:25.801
long after this is over from
everybody sending me messages.

00:08:25.801 --> 00:08:28.838
So Cyber Dust is
obviously important to me.

00:08:28.838 --> 00:08:30.850
And so, if you're able to do something

00:08:30.850 --> 00:08:32.724
that reflects something
that is important to me,

00:08:32.724 --> 00:08:34.142
or whoever it is that you're working,

00:08:34.142 --> 00:08:35.650
or trying to get a job with,

00:08:35.650 --> 00:08:36.813
or trying to get an internship with,

00:08:36.813 --> 00:08:38.787
or trying to get an investment from,

00:08:38.787 --> 00:08:40.717
if you can put yourself in their shoes,

00:08:40.717 --> 00:08:43.716
and put yourself in context
of what's important to them,

00:08:43.716 --> 00:08:48.228
your chances of being
successful increase 99%.

00:08:48.228 --> 00:08:49.676
A thousand fold.

00:08:49.676 --> 00:08:53.074
And one other point I'll leave with you.

00:08:53.074 --> 00:08:55.767
If you're working for somebody,

00:08:55.767 --> 00:08:58.074
the best way to be successful

00:08:58.074 --> 00:09:02.690
is by reducing the stress
of those around you.

00:09:02.690 --> 00:09:04.158
It's not about you.

00:09:04.158 --> 00:09:05.882
I mean, it's always about me,
but that's a different point.

00:09:05.882 --> 00:09:06.715
(audience laughter)

00:09:06.715 --> 00:09:09.821
It's not about you, it's
about the people around you.

00:09:09.821 --> 00:09:12.910
If you're reducing the stress
of the people around you,

00:09:12.910 --> 00:09:13.829
those are the people,

00:09:13.829 --> 00:09:16.825
those people start to
gravitate towards you.

00:09:16.825 --> 00:09:18.939
They want to work with you.

00:09:18.939 --> 00:09:22.360
Because you make going to
work easier, more pleasant.

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:24.050
You help them accomplish their goals.

00:09:24.050 --> 00:09:25.892
You help put them in a position to succeed

00:09:25.892 --> 00:09:27.568
by reducing their stress.

00:09:27.568 --> 00:09:29.444
You know, Shark Tank, I do the show,

00:09:29.444 --> 00:09:31.165
not because they're great investments,

00:09:31.165 --> 00:09:33.352
but I do the show because
it sends the message

00:09:33.352 --> 00:09:36.534
to kids eight to 80
that the American Dream

00:09:36.534 --> 00:09:38.034
is alive and well.

00:09:39.394 --> 00:09:41.412
You can watch the show and come to realize

00:09:41.412 --> 00:09:43.355
that somebody out of Iowa,

00:09:43.355 --> 00:09:47.325
or the middle of nowhere in
Texas can start a company,

00:09:47.325 --> 00:09:49.976
and turn it into something special.

00:09:49.976 --> 00:09:53.161
And I think so many families
watch the show together

00:09:53.161 --> 00:09:56.064
that particularly in this day and age

00:09:56.064 --> 00:09:57.786
when so many people are
saying what's wrong with this

00:09:57.786 --> 00:10:00.024
and what's wrong with that
and what's wrong with America,

00:10:00.024 --> 00:10:02.825
it just really reinforces
that somebody with an idea

00:10:02.825 --> 00:10:05.140
who's willing to go beyond just an idea

00:10:05.140 --> 00:10:07.117
to a company phase and really work,

00:10:07.117 --> 00:10:08.474
something special can happen,

00:10:08.474 --> 00:10:12.373
and just reinforcing that
is really important to me.

00:10:12.373 --> 00:10:15.520
What makes it work is like,
everybody's got ideas, right?

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:16.636
And most people just think,

00:10:16.636 --> 00:10:19.008
"oh it's my idea and I
don't have this," right?

00:10:19.008 --> 00:10:19.841
The people that get it done,

00:10:19.841 --> 00:10:21.649
they don't look at the obstacles,

00:10:21.649 --> 00:10:23.126
they look at the opportunities.

00:10:23.126 --> 00:10:24.635
Instead of coming up and saying,

00:10:24.635 --> 00:10:25.468
"Oh, I wish I had a connection.

00:10:25.468 --> 00:10:27.238
"Wish I had money, wish I had this."

00:10:27.238 --> 00:10:29.264
Like this dude with the surfboard man,

00:10:29.264 --> 00:10:31.759
he went out and found a used surfboard

00:10:31.759 --> 00:10:33.368
and he started putting it all together

00:10:33.368 --> 00:10:34.981
and it was just his time.

00:10:34.981 --> 00:10:37.218
And then he took it, built one.

00:10:37.218 --> 00:10:39.006
Figured it out, build another one.

00:10:39.006 --> 00:10:40.344
And then it's the same thing
with some of the other,

00:10:40.344 --> 00:10:42.085
pretty much all of the other companies,

00:10:42.085 --> 00:10:45.559
where, rather than finding the
excuses why they can't do it,

00:10:45.559 --> 00:10:47.199
they find the reasons to make it work,

00:10:47.199 --> 00:10:50.169
and then they just bust their
ass and good things happen.

00:10:50.169 --> 00:10:52.756
- [Announcer] Team Cuban
going for it, Bjergsn, oh!

00:10:52.756 --> 00:10:54.089
The three man pulling!

00:10:54.089 --> 00:10:56.130
And there's still more fights to happen.

00:10:56.130 --> 00:10:56.963
- [Host] No!

00:10:56.963 --> 00:10:58.461
- [Announcer] Hotshot, dodging back out.

00:10:58.461 --> 00:10:59.432
High still drops.

00:10:59.432 --> 00:11:03.343
The big, good fight for team
Cuban, an ace, actually.

00:11:03.343 --> 00:11:05.653
- [Host] Yup. And that may be.

00:11:05.653 --> 00:11:07.308
Do you think they can
close it out right now?

00:11:07.308 --> 00:11:08.981
I mean, they've got a lot of death timers.

00:11:08.981 --> 00:11:10.775
- [Announcer] Double left
alright he's on the nexus.

00:11:10.775 --> 00:11:12.297
Reginald's on the nexus too.

00:11:12.297 --> 00:11:13.273
No other's making it.

00:11:13.273 --> 00:11:14.141
- [Host] Whose going to get the last hit?

00:11:14.141 --> 00:11:15.236
Whose it going to be?

00:11:15.236 --> 00:11:16.113
- [Announcer] They don't
even know what's happening.

00:11:16.113 --> 00:11:17.363
- [Host] Is it Reggie or Tongalim?

00:11:17.363 --> 00:11:18.196
Oh, they're waiting,

00:11:18.196 --> 00:11:19.854
the snowball connects
high and couldn't come in.

00:11:19.854 --> 00:11:20.687
- [Announcer] But the minions are coming.

00:11:20.687 --> 00:11:21.658
- [Host] One more fight!

00:11:21.658 --> 00:11:23.550
And then he won the game, oh!

00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:24.383
Gigi!

00:11:24.383 --> 00:11:26.197
- [Announcer] And some impressive support

00:11:26.197 --> 00:11:28.271
from these guys for Cybersmile as well.

00:11:28.271 --> 00:11:30.146
Always fun to raise
some money for charity.

00:11:30.146 --> 00:11:32.021
(laughs)

00:11:32.021 --> 00:11:36.188
Mark Cuban clearly delighted
with his victory here.

00:11:38.695 --> 00:11:40.105
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:11:40.105 --> 00:11:41.110
I hope you enjoyed.

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:42.635
If you want to get your pick

00:11:42.635 --> 00:11:45.807
of who I should profile
next in the Top Tens,

00:11:45.807 --> 00:11:47.072
check out the link in the description

00:11:47.072 --> 00:11:48.788
and go and cast your vote.

00:11:48.788 --> 00:11:50.075
I also want to give a quick shout-out to

00:11:50.075 --> 00:11:52.742
Megan Loftin from the
Bootstrap Boutique Channel.

00:11:52.742 --> 00:11:55.225
Megan, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:11:55.225 --> 00:11:57.077
"Your One Word," and doing the review

00:11:57.077 --> 00:11:58.330
on your YouTube channel,

00:11:58.330 --> 00:12:00.543
as well as posting to
your Twitter account.

00:12:00.543 --> 00:12:01.658
I really appreciate the support

00:12:01.658 --> 00:12:03.424
and I'm glad you enjoyed the read.

00:12:03.424 --> 00:12:04.794
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:12:04.794 --> 00:12:06.149
I believe in you.

00:12:06.149 --> 00:12:08.217
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:12:08.217 --> 00:12:10.345
and whatever your one word is.

00:12:10.345 --> 00:12:12.985
Much love, I'll see you soon.

00:12:12.985 --> 00:12:15.318
(swooshing)

00:12:19.301 --> 00:12:23.181
- You know I didn't have as
many personal relationships

00:12:23.181 --> 00:12:24.947
as I probably would have liked.

00:12:24.947 --> 00:12:27.864
I put personal relationships aside.

00:12:30.717 --> 00:12:34.451
I had girlfriends where it was like,

00:12:34.451 --> 00:12:36.545
"me or your business."

00:12:36.545 --> 00:12:40.337
And I was like, "what
was your name again?"

00:12:40.337 --> 00:12:43.337
(audience laughter)

00:12:45.779 --> 00:12:46.949
You think I'm kidding.

00:12:46.949 --> 00:12:48.321
(audience laughter)

00:12:48.321 --> 00:12:52.305
So, I probably took things to an extreme.

00:12:52.305 --> 00:12:54.817
But yeah, I gave up on
personal relationships

00:12:54.817 --> 00:12:57.557
in a big respect because
I really was on a mission.

00:12:57.557 --> 00:13:00.307
And once I realized I was good...

00:13:02.551 --> 00:13:03.924
So when I sold my first company,

00:13:03.924 --> 00:13:06.171
I bought a lifetime pass
on American Airlines

00:13:06.171 --> 00:13:08.369
and my goal was just to
party like a rockstar

00:13:08.369 --> 00:13:09.202
in as many countries,

00:13:09.202 --> 00:13:11.034
with as many people as I possibly could.

00:13:11.034 --> 00:13:13.283
Then I went back and got involved
in the streaming business,

00:13:13.283 --> 00:13:15.472
and I remember sitting there and telling,

00:13:15.472 --> 00:13:17.457
we had probably 10 employees, saying,

00:13:17.457 --> 00:13:18.518
"Look, this streaming thing

00:13:18.518 --> 00:13:19.931
"I think is going to be really big.

00:13:19.931 --> 00:13:21.902
"And so, we really have to focus.

00:13:21.902 --> 00:13:24.357
"And there's going to be some sacrifices.

00:13:24.357 --> 00:13:27.326
"And if you're willing to make
those sacrifices, let's go.

00:13:27.326 --> 00:13:28.436
"And if you're not, I understand,

00:13:28.436 --> 00:13:30.301
"you make your own decisions."

00:13:30.301 --> 00:13:32.406
And I was willing to
make those sacrifices.

00:13:32.406 --> 00:13:36.073
And it all turned out
great in the long run,

00:13:37.003 --> 00:13:39.906
but during that time,
it wasn't always easy,

00:13:39.906 --> 00:13:42.614
and it wasn't always...

00:13:42.614 --> 00:13:44.548
Yeah it was tough sometimes.

00:13:44.548 --> 00:13:46.372
I always did everything
back-ass-halfwards.

00:13:46.372 --> 00:13:48.613
I like to challenge myself.

00:13:48.613 --> 00:13:51.445
And I went to Mount Lebanon
High School in Pittsburgh.

00:13:51.445 --> 00:13:54.485
And I wanted to take business
classes my junior year.

00:13:54.485 --> 00:13:56.654
Only they didn't offer
any business classes,

00:13:56.654 --> 00:13:59.855
and so I decided I'd take
some college business classes,

00:13:59.855 --> 00:14:01.047
but University of Pittsburgh

00:14:01.047 --> 00:14:03.328
didn't offer any real
business classes either.

00:14:03.328 --> 00:14:05.880
So I said I'm going to challenge myself.

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:08.924
And so, I took a couple night classes

00:14:08.924 --> 00:14:10.315
at the University of Pittsburgh

00:14:10.315 --> 00:14:11.779
my junior year in high school.

00:14:11.779 --> 00:14:12.811
Did well in them,

00:14:12.811 --> 00:14:15.461
and wasn't really challenged
all that much at all.

00:14:15.461 --> 00:14:16.986
And so I just dropped out of high school,

00:14:16.986 --> 00:14:19.005
and instead of going
back for my senior year,

00:14:19.005 --> 00:14:21.116
I went to the University of Pittsburgh

00:14:21.116 --> 00:14:24.010
and started taking classes there.

00:14:24.010 --> 00:14:25.496
(laughs)

00:14:25.496 --> 00:14:29.042
My buddies were like "Where'd you go?"

00:14:29.042 --> 00:14:30.781
I've always tried to challenge myself.

00:14:30.781 --> 00:14:32.582
You've got to know your
own skill set, right?

00:14:32.582 --> 00:14:35.068
And you've got to know how that fits

00:14:35.068 --> 00:14:37.818
within your company's life cycle.

00:14:39.078 --> 00:14:41.498
Some companies are a slow grind.

00:14:41.498 --> 00:14:42.909
And you just have to understand that,

00:14:42.909 --> 00:14:44.431
and you've just got to bide your time

00:14:44.431 --> 00:14:46.330
until it starts to click,

00:14:46.330 --> 00:14:47.806
and then grow with it quickly.

00:14:47.806 --> 00:14:49.781
If you're trying to release a product

00:14:49.781 --> 00:14:51.154
that needs to be ubiquitous,

00:14:51.154 --> 00:14:54.287
you've got to go as fast as you can

00:14:54.287 --> 00:14:55.661
and then, you know--

00:14:55.661 --> 00:14:57.211
- Release a product, like a launch?

00:14:57.211 --> 00:15:01.140
- Yeah and there's a lot
of people that'll say

00:15:01.140 --> 00:15:03.873
perfection is the enemy of profitability.

00:15:03.873 --> 00:15:04.925
And that doesn't mean you have to wait

00:15:04.925 --> 00:15:06.383
'til it's a perfect product.

00:15:06.383 --> 00:15:07.628
Cyber Dust is a perfect example.

00:15:07.628 --> 00:15:09.545
It's been almost two years,

00:15:09.545 --> 00:15:10.775
we still haven't advertised it yet,

00:15:10.775 --> 00:15:12.714
because we know it's not ready yet.

00:15:12.714 --> 00:15:14.355
And so it's been a slow growth grind

00:15:14.355 --> 00:15:16.768
because we want feedback.

00:15:16.768 --> 00:15:18.603
We want to keep on learning.

00:15:18.603 --> 00:15:20.495
We're evolving.

00:15:20.495 --> 00:15:23.281
But it really just depends
on what the product is.

00:15:23.281 --> 00:15:25.639
Is it a barbershop?

00:15:25.639 --> 00:15:26.680
Right?

00:15:26.680 --> 00:15:27.911
Is it an app?

00:15:27.911 --> 00:15:30.661
Is it a service, is it a product?

00:15:31.907 --> 00:15:35.990
But the key is looking
for the low-hanging fruit.

00:15:39.297 --> 00:15:41.418
What customers are willing
to write you a check

00:15:41.418 --> 00:15:43.124
or commit to it?

00:15:43.124 --> 00:15:45.514
So that they're willing to integrate it

00:15:45.514 --> 00:15:46.853
into their daily lives,

00:15:46.853 --> 00:15:48.882
or integrate it into their daily business.

00:15:48.882 --> 00:15:50.352
And so, getting a commitment,

00:15:50.352 --> 00:15:52.448
either through time or revenue,

00:15:52.448 --> 00:15:54.243
is typically what I look for.

00:15:54.243 --> 00:15:56.485
And so, if I can get a commitment,

00:15:56.485 --> 00:15:58.619
then I'm going to be able to learn.

00:15:58.619 --> 00:15:59.967
I'm going to see how they use it.

00:15:59.967 --> 00:16:01.458
Do they sustain usage?

00:16:01.458 --> 00:16:03.472
And then, once I get the next one,

00:16:03.472 --> 00:16:06.372
hopefully it came a little
bit faster than the first one.

00:16:06.372 --> 00:16:07.935
Then I can ask for referrals.

00:16:07.935 --> 00:16:09.645
And then the next one, then the next one.

00:16:09.645 --> 00:16:11.256
And I just try to ramp it up.

00:16:11.256 --> 00:16:12.674
You know, when I bought the Mavs,

00:16:12.674 --> 00:16:14.699
we had no season ticket holder base.

00:16:14.699 --> 00:16:18.590
And so, literally, it was a
matter of just putting a list

00:16:18.590 --> 00:16:20.237
of former season ticket holders

00:16:20.237 --> 00:16:22.140
and a white pages back then,

00:16:22.140 --> 00:16:23.999
on my desk, next to my phone,

00:16:23.999 --> 00:16:25.182
and making phone calls.

00:16:25.182 --> 00:16:26.456
- You?
- Yeah, me.

00:16:26.456 --> 00:16:27.596
Yeah because if I'm not going to do it,

00:16:27.596 --> 00:16:28.806
how can I expect someone else to do it?

00:16:28.806 --> 00:16:29.639
- That's amazing to me.

00:16:29.639 --> 00:16:30.491
So just get on the phone,

00:16:30.491 --> 00:16:31.324
"Hey, this is Mark Cuban,

00:16:31.324 --> 00:16:32.654
I'm the new owner of
the Dallas Mavericks."

00:16:32.654 --> 00:16:33.696
- Unbelievable.

00:16:33.696 --> 00:16:34.529
- I'd like to invite you back for a game.

00:16:34.529 --> 00:16:35.475
- Unbelievable.

00:16:35.475 --> 00:16:36.771
- It's not though, this is my business.

00:16:36.771 --> 00:16:38.405
- But, well, you can't get
regular sales guys or something

00:16:38.405 --> 00:16:39.859
to make those calls once
they get to a quarter.

00:16:39.859 --> 00:16:40.714
- Yeah.

00:16:40.714 --> 00:16:41.547
- You're a guy that's a billionaire,

00:16:41.547 --> 00:16:42.380
and you're making those calls.

00:16:42.380 --> 00:16:43.380
- But that's all fine and good right?

00:16:43.380 --> 00:16:45.737
Because, everybody's got
their own goals, right?

00:16:45.737 --> 00:16:48.447
But still, I didn't
want anybody at the Mavs

00:16:48.447 --> 00:16:51.981
to be able to say, "Well he's
not willing to do the work."

00:16:51.981 --> 00:16:53.499
If I walk around, I'm
picking up all the papers,

00:16:53.499 --> 00:16:54.923
I'm not saying go get that picked up.

00:16:54.923 --> 00:16:58.563
I'm like, okay that's
trash, I'm picking it up.

00:16:58.563 --> 00:17:00.410
But in terms of speed of growth,

00:17:00.410 --> 00:17:03.697
it's really, you've got to
get that first customer first.

00:17:03.697 --> 00:17:06.248
And then, when you get that
first, what did you learn?

00:17:06.248 --> 00:17:07.081
Reiterate.

00:17:07.081 --> 00:17:08.430
Get that next customer.

00:17:08.430 --> 00:17:10.542
And then, hopefully, as
you learn more and more

00:17:10.542 --> 00:17:11.826
through the process, then the next one,

00:17:11.826 --> 00:17:14.979
the next one, the next
one comes by even faster.

